\f0\fs24 \cf0 VISUAL DIRECTION \'97 DO NOT DEGRADE THE ART STYLE\
\
This is a premium-quality stylized 3D adventure game for mobile.\
\
The target is NOT "a good-looking mobile game."\
\
The target is:\
"A beautiful, highly art-directed stylized 3D adventure game that has been engineered to run on mobile."\
\
Mobile is a PERFORMANCE CONSTRAINT.\
It is NOT an ART-DIRECTION CONSTRAINT.\
\
The game should look closer to a polished modern console/PC\
storybook adventure game than to a typical mobile 3D game.\
\
The reference images establish the visual target.\
\
Do not interpret "mobile" as:\
- low-poly\
- primitive geometry\
- generic characters\
- flat lighting\
- cheap textures\
- sparse environments\
- simplified faces\
- toy-like materials\
- excessive UI\
- obvious asset-store aesthetics\
\
If something must be simplified for performance, simplify it\
in a way that is visually invisible to the player.\
\
==================================================\
CORE VISUAL PRINCIPLE\
==================================================\
\
The player should notice:\
\
beautiful characters\
beautiful lighting\
beautiful environments\
beautiful materials\
beautiful composition\
\
The player should NOT notice:\
\
polygon reduction\
texture optimization\
LOD transitions\
baked lighting\
mobile rendering limitations\
shader simplification\
\
The goal is perceived visual quality, not technical complexity.\
\
==================================================\
ART STYLE\
==================================================\
\
Use a sophisticated stylized 3D art style.\
\
The world should feel:\
\
- handcrafted\
- warm\
- inviting\
- colorful\
- believable\
- slightly whimsical\
- grounded in physical materials\
- cinematic without becoming photorealistic\
\
Geometry should have intentional stylization.\
\
Do NOT make objects look deliberately "low-poly."\
\
Do NOT add unnecessary geometric faceting simply to make the\
game look stylized.\
\
Stylization should come primarily from:\
\
- shape language\
- proportions\
- color\
- material treatment\
- lighting\
- composition\
\
rather than visibly crude geometry.\
\
==================================================\
CHARACTERS \'97 HIGHEST PRIORITY\
==================================================\
\
Character quality is critical.\
\
The player character is visible throughout the game and therefore\
must receive significantly more visual attention than ordinary props.\
\
Characters must have:\
\
- appealing facial proportions\
- expressive eyes\
- intentional facial geometry\
- attractive hair silhouettes\
- believable clothing construction\
- clean silhouettes\
- good hands and feet\
- natural proportions within the chosen stylized style\
- high-quality materials\
- readable colors\
- polished animation\
\
Faces must NOT look like generic low-poly NPC faces.\
\
Avoid:\
\
- blank faces\
- dead eyes\
- primitive noses/mouths\
- exaggerated plastic skin\
- generic mannequin geometry\
- overly simplified facial features\
- obviously procedural character construction\
\
The protagonist should look like a professionally designed game\
character even when viewed relatively close to the camera.\
\
NPCs may use progressively simplified geometry, but they must retain\
the same visual language.\
\
Use a hierarchy:\
\
PROTAGONIST\
highest quality\
\
IMPORTANT NPC\
high quality\
\
ORDINARY NPC\
moderate quality\
\
DISTANT NPC\
simplified\
\
The player should never see an obvious quality transition.\
\
==================================================\
ARCHITECTURE\
==================================================\
\
Buildings should feel handcrafted rather than modularly assembled.\
\
Use:\
\
- strong silhouettes\
- irregular but intentional construction\
- timber framing\
- stone foundations\
- plaster variation\
- roof tile variation\
- wooden beams\
- doors and windows with character\
- subtle weathering\
- vines\
- moss\
- signs\
- lanterns\
- shutters\
- flower boxes\
- small architectural details\
\
Avoid perfectly uniform surfaces.\
\
Avoid repeating the same wall/roof module everywhere.\
\
Variation should be controlled and art-directed.\
\
==================================================\
MATERIAL QUALITY\
==================================================\
\
Materials should communicate what an object is.\
\
WOOD:\
\
- visible grain\
- directional grain\
- subtle color variation\
- different roughness between old/new surfaces\
- slightly worn edges\
- occasional scratches\
\
STONE:\
\
- shape variation\
- subtle color variation\
- different roughness\
- believable mortar\
- occasional weathering\
\
PLASTER:\
\
- subtle surface variation\
- slightly uneven color\
- weathering near ground level\
- variation between buildings\
\
METAL:\
\
- correct metallic response\
- restrained highlights\
- appropriate roughness\
- subtle wear\
\
CLOTH:\
\
- matte\
- soft highlights\
- folds where visible\
- color variation between materials\
\
Do not make everything equally shiny.\
\
Do not make everything equally rough.\
\
Material response should help distinguish the world.\
\
==================================================\
LIGHTING\
==================================================\
\
Lighting should be one of the primary sources of visual quality.\
\
Target:\
\
WARM SUNLIGHT\
+\
COOL ENVIRONMENTAL FILL\
+\
SOFT SHADOWS\
+\
SUBTLE BOUNCED LIGHT\
+\
ATMOSPHERIC DEPTH\
\
Use a strong but soft directional key light.\
\
Sunlight should feel warm and inviting.\
\
Sky/environment lighting should be slightly cooler.\
\
This warm/cool contrast is intentional.\
\
Buildings should receive believable indirect illumination.\
\
Objects must feel grounded against the terrain.\
\
Avoid:\
\
- flat ambient lighting\
- uniformly bright surfaces\
- black shadows\
- excessive bloom\
- completely white sunlight\
- completely green ambient light\
- overexposed environments\
\
The scene should have clear visual hierarchy between:\
\
sunlit areas\
open shade\
deep shade\
interior spaces\
\
==================================================\
ENVIRONMENTAL DETAIL\
==================================================\
\
The world should feel lived in.\
\
Do not simply place buildings on a green terrain plane.\
\
Every major environment should contain layered detail.\
\
GROUND:\
\
- grass\
- dirt\
- worn paths\
- small stones\
- weeds\
- flowers\
- patches of exposed earth\
- subtle color variation\
\
BUILDINGS:\
\
- signs\
- crates\
- barrels\
- benches\
- tools\
- lanterns\
- carts\
- ropes\
- wood piles\
- baskets\
- small decorations\
\
Use environmental storytelling.\
\
Props should look like they belong there.\
\
Avoid random prop scattering.\
\
Everything should appear deliberately placed.\
\
==================================================\
VEGETATION\
==================================================\
\
Vegetation should be lush and attractive.\
\
Use multiple scales:\
\
FOREGROUND:\
high-quality plants, flowers, grass clumps\
\
GAMEPLAY DISTANCE:\
medium-detail vegetation\
\
BACKGROUND:\
simplified trees and vegetation\
\
Do not distribute vegetation uniformly.\
\
Create natural clusters, clearings, paths and transitions.\
\
Vegetation should frame important gameplay areas rather than obscure them.\
\
==================================================\
WORLD COMPOSITION\
==================================================\
\
Scenes should be deliberately composed.\
\
Do not simply build an environment and then put the camera inside it.\
\
Important objects should be visually discoverable.\
\
Use:\
\
- foreground framing\
- midground gameplay space\
- background scenery\
- controlled sightlines\
- color contrast\
- lighting contrast\
- depth\
\
The player should frequently see an attractive composition when moving\
through the world.\
\
The camera should make the world feel larger and more cinematic\
without becoming inconvenient for gameplay.\
\
==================================================\
CAMERA\
==================================================\
\
Use a polished third-person camera with a moderately elevated view.\
\
The camera should provide:\
\
- clear player visibility\
- clear interaction visibility\
- good view of the environment\
- attractive architectural compositions\
- moderate perspective distortion\
\
Avoid an excessively wide-angle camera.\
\
The player should feel like a character inside the world,\
not a tiny object viewed from a debugging camera.\
\
==================================================\
WATER\
==================================================\
\
Water is an important visual feature.\
\
Use a stylized but believable water material.\
\
It should have:\
\
- animated surface movement\
- subtle normal variation\
- controlled specular highlights\
- depth-dependent color\
- slightly darker deeper water\
- subtle foam where appropriate\
- believable interaction with stone channels\
\
Do not use a flat blue texture.\
\
==================================================\
ATMOSPHERE\
==================================================\
\
Use subtle atmospheric perspective to create depth.\
\
Distant objects should gradually become:\
\
- lower contrast\
- slightly less saturated\
- softer\
\
Do not use heavy fog.\
\
The player should perceive a larger world beyond the immediate\
gameplay area.\
\
==================================================\
UI\
==================================================\
\
UI should feel like part of the same premium art direction.\
\
Use:\
\
- dark translucent surfaces\
- warm cream/white typography\
- restrained gold accents\
- subtle borders\
- rounded shapes\
- polished icons\
- consistent spacing\
- subtle animation\
\
The UI should be elegant rather than "mobile-app-like."\
\
Dialogue should feel cinematic.\
\
Interaction markers should be integrated into the world.\
\
Do not cover the environment unnecessarily.\
\
==================================================\
INTERACTION MARKERS\
==================================================\
\
World interactions should use a consistent visual language.\
\
For example:\
\
small icon\
+\
short label\
+\
subtle outline/glow\
+\
optional world-space connector\
\
Examples:\
\
"Rope \'b7 Salvage"\
\
"2 boards + rope \'b7 Jump"\
\
"Move cargo"\
\
"Repair cart"\
\
The marker should clearly identify the object without looking like\
debug text.\
\
==================================================\
MOBILE OPTIMIZATION\
==================================================\
\
Optimize invisibly.\
\
Prioritize:\
\
- baked lighting where appropriate\
- efficient materials\
- texture atlases\
- sensible texture resolutions\
- mesh LODs\
- instancing\
- visibility culling\
- efficient vegetation\
- limited dynamic lights\
- limited transparent materials\
- controlled shadow distance\
\
Do NOT lower visual quality merely because the game is mobile.\
\
Instead determine what the player actually sees and spend the\
performance budget there.\
\
Spend the highest visual budget on:\
\
1. protagonist\
2. nearby NPCs\
3. gameplay objects\
4. buildings\
5. nearby vegetation\
6. lighting/shadows\
7. UI\
\
Spend less on:\
\
- distant geometry\
- distant vegetation\
- unseen surfaces\
- background NPCs\
- tiny objects\
\
==================================================\
QUALITY BAR\
==================================================\
\
Before accepting an asset or scene, ask:\
\
"Does this look intentionally designed?"\
\
"Does this look like it belongs in the same game as the\
reference images?"\
\
"Does this look good when the player gets close?"\
\
"Does the lighting make the object feel integrated into the world?"\
\
"Does the character look professionally designed?"\
\
"Does the environment feel lived in?"\
\
If the answer is no, do not compensate by adding more objects.\
\
Fix the underlying:\
\
- model\
- material\
- lighting\
- composition\
- proportions\
- texture\
- animation\
\
QUALITY OVER QUANTITY.\
\
==================================================\
ABSOLUTE DO-NOT-DO LIST\
==================================================\
\
Do NOT create:\
\
- cheap-looking low-poly characters\
- generic humanoid faces\
- dead eyes\
- flat lighting\
- uniform green terrain\
- generic asset-store villages\
- repetitive buildings\
- uniformly scattered vegetation\
- plastic-looking wood\
- shiny stone\
- flat blue water\
- excessive bloom\
- heavy fog\
- debug-looking interaction labels\
- UI that looks disconnected from the world\
- unnecessary geometry purely for polygon count\
- visual effects added just because they are technically available\
\
The finished game should look like a carefully art-directed premium\
stylized adventure game that has been intelligently optimized for mobile.\
\
NEVER let "mobile optimization" become visible as "cheap art."}